  <h2>What to record while you work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The tool and the model, with its version.</li>
    <li>What you asked it to do: summarise, suggest, translate, draft.</li>
    <li>Which parts of your work it affected.</li>
    <li>How you checked its output against sources.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A disclosure you can adapt</h2>
  <p class="card">
    “We used [tool] with [model and version] to [purpose]. All outputs were checked against the cited sources by the
    authors, who take full responsibility for the content.”
  </p>

  <h2>Why checking matters</h2>
  <p>
    Models can invent citations and misattribute claims, the problem known as
    <a href={href('glossary/hallucination/')}>hallucination</a>. Disclosing use is not enough: each claim that came from
    an AI should be traced to its source. See <a href={href('guides/check-ai-answers-against-sources/')}>how to check AI
    answers</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
